# TEMPLATE — not applied, not encrypted, not read by anything. It exists so the
# shape of the real file is reviewable in git while the real file is opaque
# ciphertext. Copy it to `alertmanager-config.sops.yaml`, fill the two
# placeholders, encrypt with `sops -e -i`, and commit the encrypted result
# alongside this template (which stays, as the diffable reference).
#
# The whole Alertmanager configuration lives inside one Secret value rather
# than in the chart's `alertmanager.config` values block for two reasons. The
# bot token is a credential, so it cannot sit in a committed valuesObject. And
# `chat_id` has to be a bare integer: Telegram group ids are negative all-digit
# numbers, Alertmanager's parser is strict and rejects a quoted one, while this
# repo's rule that every SOPS value must be a quoted string binds the *outer*
# `alertmanager.yaml` value — a block scalar, which is a string — and says
# nothing about the YAML nested inside it. Keeping the config in the Secret is
# what lets both rules hold at once.
#
# ⚠ When editing the real file with `sops`, the value under `alertmanager.yaml`
# must stay a block scalar (`|`). If it ever gets re-typed as a plain flow
# scalar the Secret still encrypts and still applies, and Alertmanager then
# fails to parse it at startup.
#
# Hand-writing this config means opting out of the chart's default one, and
# the chart's default is not just a route to `null` — it also carries four
# inhibit rules that exist to tame the rules the chart itself ships. Two of
# them are about `InfoInhibitor`, a synthetic alert in the `general.rules`
# group whose own description reads "This alert should be routed to a null
# receiver and configured to inhibit alerts with severity=info". It fires
# whenever any `severity: info` alert is firing in a namespace with no
# warning/critical alongside it, and 87.21.0 ships seven info-level alerts
# that a home lab trips routinely — CPUThrottlingHigh, KubeletTooManyPods,
# NodeCPUHighUsage, KubeNodePressure, KubeNodeEviction, KubeQuotaAlmostFull,
# KubeQuotaFullyUsed. Miss it and Telegram gets a meaningless "Info-level
# alert inhibition" message every 12h forever. Note it carries
# `severity: none`, not `info`, so no severity-based rule or route catches it
# by accident — it has to be named. It is named twice below: matched onto the
# `null` route (unconditional, and the reason the chart's fourth default rule
# — an inhibit rule with an empty source, relying on the always-firing
# Watchdog to suppress it — is not reproduced here), and kept as an inhibit
# *source* so the info alerts it stands in for are suppressed too.
#
# Validated against Alertmanager v0.33.1 (the version kube-prometheus-stack
# 87.21.0 deploys) with `amtool check-config`. Alertmanager unmarshals its
# config strictly — an unknown or misspelled key is a hard startup failure, not
# a warning — so any edit here should be re-checked the same way. Note that
# `check-config` validates syntax only: it accepted the earlier version of
# this file that had none of the InfoInhibitor handling at all.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: alertmanager-config
  namespace: monitoring
type: Opaque
stringData:
  alertmanager.yaml: |
    global:
      resolve_timeout: 5m
    route:
      receiver: telegram
      # Grouped by alert and namespace so one broken namespace is one thread,
      # not one message per firing series. 12h repeat: long enough that a
      # known-unfixed alert is not noise, short enough to still nag.
      group_by: [alertname, namespace]
      group_wait: 30s
      group_interval: 5m
      repeat_interval: 12h
      routes:
        # Two synthetic alerts the chart ships that must never reach Telegram.
        # Watchdog is the always-firing heartbeat, so you can tell "no alerts"
        # from "alerting is dead". InfoInhibitor is the info-suppression
        # sentinel described in the header. Both would otherwise page every
        # 12h forever — the root route's receiver is `telegram`, so anything
        # not caught here is delivered.
        - receiver: "null"
          matchers:
            - alertname =~ "Watchdog|InfoInhibitor"
    receivers:
      - name: "null"
      - name: telegram
        telegram_configs:
          # api_url defaults to https://api.telegram.org — not set on purpose.
          # bot_token_file is the alternative to bot_token; it is not used here
          # because the token is already delivered as a Secret.
          - bot_token: <paste-bot-token-here>
            # Bare integer, unquoted, negative for a group — e.g. -1001234567890
            chat_id: <paste-chat-id-here>
            parse_mode: HTML
            send_resolved: true
    # The chart's own defaults, restated because hand-writing this config
    # replaces them wholesale. Dropping any one of them is a silent noise
    # regression, not an error.
    inhibit_rules:
      # While a critical is firing for some alertname+namespace, suppress the
      # quieter twins rather than sending all three.
      - source_matchers:
          - severity = "critical"
        target_matchers:
          - severity =~ "warning|info"
        equal: [alertname, namespace]
      - source_matchers:
          - severity = "warning"
        target_matchers:
          - severity = "info"
        equal: [alertname, namespace]
      # The point of InfoInhibitor: it fires exactly when a namespace has
      # info-level alerts and nothing more serious, so using it as a source
      # holds back the info alerts until something real joins them. Equal on
      # namespace only — the whole point is to inhibit *other* alertnames.
      - source_matchers:
          - alertname = "InfoInhibitor"
        target_matchers:
          - severity = "info"
        equal: [namespace]
